# Plain .trivyignore — this is trivy's own DEFAULT ignorefile (repo root,
# `--ignorefile` default), auto-loaded by every gate that doesn't override it.
# In this repo that's the org-shared `scan / Vulnerability Scan (Trivy)` gate
# (.github/workflows/security.yml → ADORSYS-GIS/ai-governance
# security-gates.yml, pinned SHA, `scanners: vuln,secret,misconfig`) — it
# passes NO `trivyignores`/`ignorefile` input to aquasecurity/trivy-action, so
# trivy falls back to this file. It's DIFFERENT from .trivyignore.yaml (whose
# `paths:` scheme is `rendered/<chart>.yaml`, a single flat file per chart —
# matches ONLY release-helm-charts.yml's deterministic scan, which renders
# each chart to `rendered/<chart>.yaml` before scanning; it structurally
# cannot match paths this gate reports, which are directory-relative,
# since this gate scans `charts/` directly with trivy's own Helm renderer).
#
# ⚠️ This trivy version's plain .trivyignore is ID-ONLY for misconfigurations
# — no path-scoping (tested: a trailing path token on the line is silently
# ignored, verified empirically against a wrong path). Any entry here is
# therefore a genuine BLANKET ignore of the rule ID, not scoped to one file,
# unlike .trivyignore.yaml's policy of "never a blanket whole-ID ignore" — the
# tool doesn't give this gate that option.
#
# ⚠️ This gate can only render DEPENDENCY-FREE charts — it does a bare
# checkout with no `helm dependency build`, so any chart with a subchart
# dependency fails to render and is silently SKIPPED, never reaching this
# ignorefile at all. A blanket entry here therefore only has any effect for
# whichever dependency-free chart currently trips that ID — check that before
# adding one, or you're carrying dead risk (an ignore with no coverage, but
# still hiding a real finding repo-wide the day some OTHER dependency-free
# chart legitimately trips the same ID).
#
# No active entries as of 2026-08-08. Previously carried KSV-0005/0014/0118
# for charts/librechat-code-interpreter's sandbox-runner controller
# (ADR-0122) while that chart was dependency-free; removed once it gained
# the bjw-template + common dependencies (Chart.yaml) — those findings are
# still legitimately exempt, but now only reachable via release-helm-
# charts.yml's deterministic scan, which .trivyignore.yaml already covers
# (caught in PR review — the entries here had silently stopped providing any
# coverage for the chart they named, and were dead weight carrying only the
# blind-spot risk described above).
